#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2835d0-7988-50d7-ae7c-db38495e1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(\\sin (2))\\sin (2πx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230308} {:answer/id #uuid "37af8f65-b31c-5d95-8130-da3366f434bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(\\cos (\\sqrt{2}))\\cos (\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230309} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd9aa25-0ee8-5ea7-b34f-5991d4138f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(\\sin (\\sqrt{2}))\\sin (\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230310} {:answer/id #uuid "70cd6e17-e444-58f3-bf73-8607a835febb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(\\cos (2))\\cos (2πx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230311}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1155ce63-e95f-57ed-874b-ccec97631f5d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230307} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71a6be11-8878-5070-81b7-b47676c4ce8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385292} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce9062b-d93f-55c2-a03a-1ae8c327c26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385293} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa04091-bc19-5979-8a5e-2e3897f35181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385294} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee0778f-6531-5ab8-bddf-60696be3a1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ee0778f-6531-5ab8-bddf-60696be3a1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385295} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ecdd4ec-27ca-5ae4-9688-c8b8aa4f79ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a08e9ea-1ab7-5988-bf4e-d55c1dd407e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343668" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=3$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 3 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d9d35e-90ec-5eca-be92-51a8daa58518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230318} {:answer/id #uuid "60153d96-24bc-578b-9e10-f97d8db3f8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230319} {:answer/id #uuid "66aea4ec-1698-5e28-9d01-23bbb3577463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230320} {:answer/id #uuid "783ccf8f-7573-5c2e-a7b8-786a6c20e84b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230321}] :answer-field/id #uuid "820b3701-ddd6-58d9-9b7a-77aa5711efbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230317}] :question/id #uuid "3ccc73a6-d864-55e4-9da3-f78dd465c648" :question/math-academy-id "q-343671" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} + 3y = f(x)$\non $[- 3, 3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution $y_{p}$?\n$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1db83d-b0ff-520c-a225-6d525cff002b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372856} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8ea9a4-c740-500c-9fab-6d37ceab5899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372857} {:answer/id #uuid "7e24b0bc-eb98-5df7-b3c2-9e5493a8cc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372858} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6e5693-da89-597a-8638-edeca9949654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e24b0bc-eb98-5df7-b3c2-9e5493a8cc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372858} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e4c22e-78c5-5e23-8e3c-f648b14c9d5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31df3052-c15b-5b42-8dc7-4a21ca73beb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344267" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-4,4]$, where $f$ is $8$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $8$-periodic on $[-4,4]$, we have $L=4$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1ee564-8e0f-5ee0-998f-2101fc9cd57a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372880} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1903c2-0865-5c88-ad0e-67617d3c44ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372881} {:answer/id #uuid "6005d9a8-46b9-5819-acc2-87a742a05929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372882} {:answer/id #uuid "3078353a-b59d-5067-b6f6-a1a830986836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3078353a-b59d-5067-b6f6-a1a830986836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372883} :answer-field/id #uuid "01f8df2d-ecbb-512a-aa1b-2ac650da1f39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b96aedd-ce80-5f05-9df5-d9873f821b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-344321" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=4$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 4 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}]}
